      "text": "The term 'woke' begins to be used in African-American Vernacular English to refer to an awareness of social and political issues affecting African Americans."
    },
    {
      "date": "1962",
      "headline": "First Recorded Use in Print",
      "text": "The term 'woke' is first recorded in print in a 1962 New York Times Magazine article by William Melvin Kelley, titled 'If You're Woke You Dig It,' discussing African-American slang."
    },
    {
      "date": "2008",
      "headline": "Erykah Badu's Song 'Master Teacher'",
      "text": "The phrase 'stay woke' is popularized by Erykah Badu in her song 'Master Teacher,' which brings the term into broader cultural awareness."
    },
    {
      "date": "2014",
      "headline": "Rise in Popularity",
      "text": "The term 'woke' gains widespread usage following the Black Lives Matter movement, particularly after the shooting of Michael Brown in Ferguson, Missouri. It becomes associated with social justice and activism."
    },
    {
      "date": "2016",
      "headline": "Oxford Dictionaries Shortlist",
      "text": "The term 'woke' is shortlisted by Oxford Dictionaries as the Word of the Year, reflecting its growing prominence in discussions of social justice."
    },
    {
      "date": "2017",
      "headline": "Addition to Oxford English Dictionary",
      "text": "The term 'woke' is officially added to the Oxford English Dictionary, defined as 'alert to injustice in society, especially racism.'"
    },
    {
      "date": "2020",
      "headline": "Cultural and Political Debates",
      "text": "The term 'woke' becomes a focal point in cultural and political debates, often used pejoratively by critics to describe perceived overzealousness in social justice movements."
    }
  ]
}

