      "date": "1815-06-18",
      "headline": "French Army Deployment",
      "text": "Napoleon deployed his forces with the intention of splitting the British and Prussian armies. His strategy involved attacking the British forces first and then turning to face the Prussians."
    },
    {
      "date": "1815-06-18",
      "headline": "Wellington's Defensive Position",
      "text": "The Duke of Wellington chose a strong defensive position on the Mont-Saint-Jean escarpment, with his troops hidden behind the ridge, which provided protection from artillery fire."
    },
    {
      "date": "1815-06-18",
      "headline": "Prussian Arrival",
      "text": "The Prussian army, under Field Marshal Blücher, arrived on the battlefield in the late afternoon, providing crucial reinforcements to the Coalition forces and attacking the French right flank."
    },
    {
      "date": "1815-06-18",
      "headline": "Napoleon's Final Attack",
      "text": "Napoleon launched a final attack with his Imperial Guard, which was repelled by the British forces. This marked the turning point of the battle, leading to a Coalition victory."
    },
    {
      "date": "1815-06-18",
      "headline": "French Retreat",
      "text": "Following the failure of the Imperial Guard's attack, the French army began a retreat, which soon turned into a rout as Coalition forces pursued them."
    },
    {
      "date": "1815-06-18",
      "headline": "End of the Napoleonic Wars",
      "text": "The defeat at Waterloo ended Napoleon's rule as Emperor of the French and marked the end of the Napoleonic Wars. Napoleon abdicated four days later and was exiled to the island of Saint Helena."
    }
  ]
}

